<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Miroslav of Hum</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 1199</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Miroslav (died c. 1199) was prince of Hum (Zachlumia), on the southern Adriatic coast, from the 1160s and a brother of the Serbian grand prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-nemanja">Stefan Nemanja</a>. He commissioned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miroslav-gospel">Miroslav Gospel</a>, one of the oldest and finest Serbian Cyrillic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, and built churches, among them that of Saints Peter and Paul at Bijelo Polje. He fought to defend his lands against Ragusa and the Bosnian ban.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/miroslav-of-hum.jpg" alt="Miroslav of Hum" width="320" height="421" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Miroslavs_Gospel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Miroslav Zavidović was a son of Zavida, a Serbian royal of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vukanovic-dynasty">Vukanović dynasty</a> who had briefly ruled as Prince of Zahumlje. His brothers were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-nemanja">Stefan Nemanja</a>, Tihomir and Stracimir. Miroslav received Zahumlje as an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a> of the Grand Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, covering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herzegovina">Herzegovina</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a>, with his seat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ston">Ston</a>. In 1166 Stefan Nemanja rebelled against the eldest brother, Tihomir, who fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> with Stracimir and Miroslav. Nemanja defeated their Byzantine mercenaries near Pantino in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a>, where Tihomir drowned in the Sitnica. The brothers lost their titles, but Nemanja pardoned them and they went on ruling their lands under him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Miroslav married a sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ban-kulin">Ban Kulin</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a>. After the death of Emperor Manuel he took under his protection the Narentine Kačić family, who had organised the murder of Rajneri, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Split, and he kept the bishopric's money; reluctant to let <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> prosper in his lands, he was excommunicated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">Papacy</a> in 1181, and the Bishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ston">Ston</a> left his seat, which has remained vacant since.</p><p>In 1184 Miroslav set out to retake the islands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korcula">Korčula</a> and Vis, but on 18 August 1184 his fleet was destroyed by the Ragusian navy at Poljice near Koločep. A peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubrovnik">Dubrovnik</a> in 1186 was followed by cordial relations, and on 27 September 1186 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-nemanja">Stefan Nemanja</a> and Miroslav signed a treaty with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a>, who held Korčula, by which Hum renounced its claims to Korčula and Vis. In 1190-1192 Nemanja gave Hum to his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-sava">Rastko Nemanjić</a>, the later Saint Sava, while Miroslav held the Lim region with Bijelo Polje; when Rastko took monastic vows, Miroslav ruled Hum again.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Miroslav died of old age. He had two sons by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ban-kulin">Ban Kulin</a>'s sister, Andrija Miroslavljević and Toljen Miroslavljević, who married a daughter of Berthold of Andechs, and possibly a third, Petar. To his church of Saints Peter and Paul in Bijelo Polje, on the Lim, he gave 20 villages. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miroslav-gospel">Miroslav Gospel</a>, the oldest known book written in Serbian in Cyrillic, was made for him and in his honour.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Miroslav of Hum” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Prince Miroslav</b>, brother of Stefan Nemanja, ruled it in the late 12th century and commissioned the Miroslav Gospel, a masterpiece of Cyrillic illumination.</blockquote><a href="/state/zachlumia">Zachlumia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/podgorica">Podgorica</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/montenegro">Montenegro</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.44,19.26&amp;year=1189" aria-label="Podgorica on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5017 654 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Miroslav of Hum"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5467 924h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5467 924h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.44,19.26&amp;year=1189">What was here in 1189? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
